A destroy that revokes only the capabilities naming its objects
leaves the slots of the dying tables in the rings of slots that survive,
and those rings then lead into memory that is user memory again.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-grain32 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: child link to a slot that is not a live, filled one

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -279,7 +279,7 @@ void cap_revoke_range(uint32_t base, uint32_t size)
             struct captable *table = (struct captable *)o;
             for (uint32_t i = 0; i < table->nslots; i++) {
                 LOOP_WALK(cap_revoke_range);
-                sweep_node(&table->slots[i], dying, base, size);
+                sweep_node(&table->slots[i], false, base, size);
             }
         } else if (o->type == CAP_PROCESS) {
             /* A living process loses its table with the table's pool; its regions name no object. */
